<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The fight's numbers (docs/design/33-combat.md section 1), in one record, from the combat
  contracts step. Which figures are the owner's and which are invented is said beside each.

  Hit points everywhere are in THOUSANDTHS of a point, the Rates convention, except where a Def
  names whole points (SpeciesDef.healthPoints, AttackDef.damage): a heal of a fraction of a point
  a tick is then exact without a float.
-->
<Defs>

  <CombatDef>
    <defName>Combat_Default</defName>

    <!--
      The attacker's chance to land, per mille, by melee level - the owner's three points
      (2026-09-23): 50 per cent at level 0, 80 at 10, 90 at 20. Linear between them, flat past the
      ends. An animal reads it at its species' meleeSkill.
    -->
    <hitCurve>
      <li><level>0</level><perMille>500</perMille></li>
      <li><level>10</level><perMille>800</perMille></li>
      <li><level>20</level><perMille>900</perMille></li>
    </hitCurve>

    <!--
      The defender's chance to step out of a swing that would have landed, per mille, by the
      DEFENDER's melee level - the owner's three points: 0 at level 0, 10 per cent at 10, 30 at 20.
      A second roll, made only after the first lands.
    -->
    <dodgeCurve>
      <li><level>0</level><perMille>0</perMille></li>
      <li><level>10</level><perMille>100</perMille></li>
      <li><level>20</level><perMille>300</perMille></li>
    </dodgeCurve>

    <!--
      A person's bare hands: about 4 damage every two seconds (owner; the reference's unarmed human
      is about 4.1 every 2 s). The wind-up is INVENTED - a punch lands about a third of a second
      in - and so is the experience, which is the weapons' 50 points a swing (Items.xml).
    -->
    <fists>
      <damage>4</damage>
      <cooldownTicks>120</cooldownTicks>
      <windupTicks>18</windupTicks>
      <damageKind>Blunt</damageKind>
      <experiencePerSwing>50000</experiencePerSwing>
      <style>Fists</style>
    </fists>

    <!-- A landed blow does its weapon's figure give or take a fifth (design 33 section 3). -->
    <damageSpreadPerMille>200</damageSpreadPerMille>

    <!--
      Healing, in thousandths of a point per in-game day. The shape is the owner's - a colonist
      heals only in a bed, an animal recovers on its own - and the rates are INVENTED: a colonist
      at nought is on her feet in about a day in bed, and a downed hog in about a day wherever it
      fell.
    -->
    <bedHealPerDay>20000</bedHealPerDay>
    <animalHealPerDay>12000</animalHealPerDay>

    <!--
      A downed pawn gets up at 150 per mille of its pool: above nought on purpose, so the first
      blow after it rises does not put it straight back down. INVENTED.
    -->
    <downedRecoverAtPerMille>150</downedRecoverAtPerMille>

    <!--
      Treatment (design 37 section 4). The heal itself is the item's (Item_MedicalSupplies'
      healPerUnit, 40); these say how far it may go and who may give it.
      treatCapPerMille 800: a treatment never lifts a pawn past four fifths of its pool, so the
        last fifth is bed rest's (owner).
      selfHealPerMille 500 and selfCapPerMille 600: treating yourself heals half, never past 60 per
        cent (owner). selfWorkFactor 3: and takes three times as long (owner).
      bareHeal 10: a doctor with no supplies dresses the wound with what is at hand (owner's "about
        +10"), under the same cap.
      patientBelowPerMille 500 and patientReleasePerMille 800: a colonist under half goes to bed,
        and gets up at the treatment cap (INVENTED).
      treatedCooldownTicks 15000: a quarter of a day before the same pawn may be treated again, so
        a pile of supplies cannot stand in for rest (INVENTED).
    -->
    <treatCapPerMille>800</treatCapPerMille>
    <selfHealPerMille>500</selfHealPerMille>
    <selfCapPerMille>600</selfCapPerMille>
    <selfWorkFactor>3</selfWorkFactor>
    <bareHeal>10</bareHeal>
    <patientBelowPerMille>500</patientBelowPerMille>
    <patientReleasePerMille>800</patientReleasePerMille>
    <treatedCooldownTicks>15000</treatedCooldownTicks>

    <!-- A chase re-plans to a moving target once a second. INVENTED; a cost figure as much as a feel. -->
    <chaseRepathTicks>60</chaseRepathTicks>

    <!--
      How long an attack nobody ordered (a hunt, a revenge, a self-defence) keeps its first target
      before it thinks again, so a bandit notices a nearer colonist. INVENTED: five seconds.
    -->
    <rechooseTicks>300</rechooseTicks>

    <!--
      How near a fight must be for a drafted colonist holding her ground to join it (design 33
      section 15; owner, 2026-09-24: "if there is any fight going on nearby ... they will help"):
      the colonist being attacked and her attacker both within this many cells. INVENTED: 20 m.
    -->
    <helpRadiusCells>8</helpRadiusCells>

    <!--
      How long a colonist struck by a colonist fights back (owner: she does), 20 seconds; and how
      long a hog that turned stays vengeful, the reference's 10,000-tick floor (a-10). Both in
      ticks. The first is INVENTED.
    -->
    <retaliationTicks>1200</retaliationTicks>
    <revengeTicks>10000</revengeTicks>

    <!-- How far a hurt animal runs when it does not turn. INVENTED: out of reach, not off the board. -->
    <fleeCells>12</fleeCells>

    <!--
      Critical blows and knockback (design 33 section 9b), the owner's numbers (2026-09-23). A blow
      that lands is critical at 10 per cent plus 1 per cent per four whole melee levels of the
      attacker (an animal counts its species' meleeSkill), and does half as much again. A critical
      knocks its target back a tile half the time, three times in four for a blunt weapon, and a
      target knocked back lies where it landed for about a second and a half (90 ticks) before it
      stands.
    -->
    <critChancePerMille>100</critChancePerMille>
    <critPerMillePerFourLevels>10</critPerMillePerFourLevels>
    <critDamagePerMille>1500</critDamagePerMille>
    <knockbackPerMille>500</knockbackPerMille>
    <knockbackBluntPerMille>750</knockbackBluntPerMille>
    <knockedDownTicks>90</knockedDownTicks>

    <!--
      The gun (design 47 sections 2a, 2c, 3b). The hit chance is shootingPerCell at the shooter's
      level, raised to the distance in 2.5 m cells, times the gun's own accuracy at that distance,
      floored at hitFloorPerMille. RAISED 2026-09-25 on the owner's first play ("seemed to miss a
      lot ... keep it more accurate"): the reference's 747 / 903 / 951 left a level-0 colonist at
      16 per cent at 12.5 m and 2 at 25 m, and fire at will engages anything in range. Now 876 /
      943 / 983, which with the pistol's bands gives about 45 / 64 / 79 per cent at 12.5 m and
      17 / 36 / 55 at 25 m: skill still buys the reach. OURS, tunable here. The floor is the
      reference's 2 per cent.
    -->
    <shootingPerCell>
      <li><level>0</level><perMille>876</perMille></li>
      <li><level>10</level><perMille>943</perMille></li>
      <li><level>20</level><perMille>983</perMille></li>
    </shootingPerCell>
    <hitFloorPerMille>20</hitFloorPerMille>
    <!-- No partial cover (owner): a wall blocks the line or it does not. The slot a cover value reads. -->
    <coverPerMille>1000</coverPerMille>
    <!--
      A bystander on the bullet's line: never hit within 5 m of the shooter, the full species
      chance from 12 m, linear between - the reference's 5 and 12 tiles, kept in metres.
    -->
    <interceptDeadZoneMm>5000</interceptDeadZoneMm>
    <interceptFullMm>12000</interceptFullMm>
    <!--
      A miss carries on past its target along the line of fire, by r cells, r = min(3, 1 +
      (1000 - hit) x 3 / 1000): one cell for a good shot, three for a hopeless one, and it ends
      where the line stops - a wall, the ground (owner, 2026-09-25: misses "way off" and "not even
      in a place a gun would fire to" were the old box round the target). Both 3s are INVENTED.
    -->
    <scatterMaxCells>3</scatterMaxCells>
    <scatterPerMissPerMille>3</scatterPerMissPerMille>
    <!-- How often a drafted gun-holder looks for something in sight to shoot. INVENTED; R5 measures it. -->
    <rangedScanTicks>15</rangedScanTicks>
  </CombatDef>

</Defs>
